The types of works that can be protected by copyright in Nigeria are:

(a) literary works;  
(b) musical works;  
(c) artistic works;  
(d) audiovisual works;  
(e) sound recordings; and  
(f) broadcasts.  

This is stated in s.2(1).

You have the exclusive right to do and authorise the doing of any of the following acts in relation to your literary or musical work:

(a) reproduce the work;  
(b) publish the work;  
(c) perform the work in public;  
(d) produce, reproduce, perform or publish any translation of the work;  
(e) make any audiovisual work or a record in respect of the work;  
(f) distribute to the public, for commercial purposes, copies of the work, through sale or other transfer of ownership provided the work has not been subject to distribution authorised by you;  
(g) broadcast the work;  
(h) communicate the work to the public;  
(i) make the work available to the public by wire or wireless means in such a way that members of the public are able to access the work from a place and at a time independently chosen by them;  
(j) make any adaptation of the work; and  
(k) do in relation to a translation or an adaptation of the work, any of the acts specified in relation to the work specified under this section.  

(s.9)

A creator has the exclusive right to do and authorise the doing of the following acts over their artistic work: 

(a) reproduce the work;  
(b) publish the work;  
(c) include the work in an audiovisual work;  
(d) broadcast the work;  
(e) communicate the work to the public;  
(f) make the work available to the public by wire or wireless means in such a way that members of the public are able to access the work from a place and at a time individually chosen by them;  
(g) make any adaptation of the work; and  
(h) do in relation to an adaptation of the work any of the acts specified in relation to the work under subsection (1)(a), (b) and (c).  

This is outlined in s.10(1).

Copyright in an audiovisual work allows you to do and authorise the doing of any of the following acts:

(a) reproduce the audiovisual work;  
(b) cause the audiovisual work that consists of visual images to be seen in public and of sounds to be heard in public;  
(c) communicate the audiovisual work to the public;  
(d) broadcast the audiovisual work;  
(e) make any copy of the sound track of an audiovisual work;  
(f) make the work available to the public by wire or wireless means in such a way that members of the public are able to access the work from a place and at a time independently chosen by them;  
(g) distribute to the public, for commercial purposes, copies of the work, through sale or other transfer of ownership provided the work has not been subject to distribution authorised by the owner;  
(h) make an adaptation of the audiovisual work;  
(i) make a translation of the audiovisual work or any part; and  
(j) do in relation to a translation or an adaptation of the work, any of the acts specified in relation to the work under this section.  

(s.11)

Under these provisions, archives, libraries, museums, and galleries can make the following types of copies:

(a) Copies of works protected under this Act as part of their ordinary activities for non-commercial purposes (s.25(1)(a));

(b) Copies of works in their collection for the purpose of back-up and preservation (s.25(1)(b));

(c) Copies of any missing part of a work in its collection from another institution (s.25(1)(c));

(d) Copies of any work that is or should be available in its collection in any chosen format, where the work cannot reasonably be acquired in that format through general trade or from the publisher (s.25(1)(d));

(e) Copies of any work where the permission of the owner of the copyright cannot be obtained, after reasonable effort, or where the work is not available by general trade or from the publisher (s.25(1)(e)).

The Commission considers the following factors when deciding to grant permission to use a work:

(a) The proposed user has made reasonable effort to obtain permission from the owner of copyright on reasonable commercial terms and conditions and that the effort was not successful;  
(b) The scope, medium and duration of the use shall be limited to the purpose for which it was authorised;  
(c) The use shall be non-exclusive and non-assignable;  
(d) The use shall be exclusively for Nigeria’s domestic market; and  
(e) The copyright owner receives payment of adequate remuneration, taking into account the economic value of the work authorised to be used.  

These factors are outlined in s.35(3).

The actions that can violate someone's copyright without their permission include:

(a) Doing or causing any person to do an act that constitutes a violation of the exclusive rights conferred under this Act;

(b) Importing or causing to be imported into Nigeria any copy of a work which if it had been made in Nigeria would be an infringing copy under this Act;

(c) Selling, offering for sale or hire any work in respect of which copyright is infringed under paragraph (a);

(d) Making or having in possession, plates, master tapes, machines, equipment or contrivances used for the sole purpose of making infringing copies of the work;

(e) Permitting a place of public entertainment or of business to be used for a public performance of the work, where the performance constitutes an infringement of copyright in the work, unless the person permitting the place to be used was not aware and had no reasonable ground to suspect that the performance constitutes an infringement of the copyright;

(f) Permitting within its premises, the reproduction of a copyright work; or

(g) Performing or causing to be performed for the purposes of trade or business or the promotion of a trade or business, any work in which copyright subsists. 

(2) The doing of any of the acts referred to in this section shall be in respect of the whole or a substantial part of the work either in its original form or in any form recognisably derived from the original. 

Cited as s.36.

According to s.42 of the Nigerian Copyright Act 2022, the following facts can be accepted in court without needing extra proof if they are stated in an affidavit made before a competent authority:

(a) at the time specified, copyright subsists in the work;  
(b) the person named is the owner of the copyright in the work;  
(c) a copy of the work exhibited to the affidavit is a true copy of the work;  
(d) the author of the work is a citizen of or domiciled in a country named in the affidavit;  
(e) the author of the work is a body corporate established or incorporated by or under the laws of the country named in the affidavit;  
(f) the work was first made or published in the country named in the affidavit; and  
(g) the certificate attached to the affidavit is a true copy of the certificate or registration of that incorporation.

Registration of your work under section 87 of the Nigerian Copyright Act 2022 helps your case in an infringement action by providing several presumptions as outlined in s.43. Specifically, it presumes that:

(a) copyright subsists in your work, which is crucial for establishing your rights;
(b) the name appearing on your work as the author is recognized as such;
(c) the name on the work as the publisher or producer is acknowledged as that entity;
(d) if you are deceased, the work is considered original; and
(e) it is presumed that the work was published or produced at the place and on the date indicated.

These presumptions strengthen your position in proving infringement.

If a work is registered under the copyright law, you can assume the following: 

(a) that copyright subsists in the work which is the subject matter of an alleged infringement;  
(b) that the name appearing on the work purporting to be the name of the author is the name of such author;  
(c) that the name appearing on the work purporting to be that of the publisher or producer of the work is the name of such publisher or producer;  
(d) where the author is dead, that the work is an original work; and  
(e) that it was published or produced at the place and on the date appearing on the work.  

(s.43)

You need to record the following information about a work: 

(a) name of the author;  
(b) title of the work;  
(c) date of use or production;  
(d) quantity of the work used or produced; and  
(e) any other information as may be prescribed by the Commission. 

This is outlined in s.48(1).

To notify a service provider about copyright infringement, you must issue a notice in writing, which can be transmitted electronically or by other means, to the service provider or their designated agent. The notice must include the following elements as outlined in s.54(2):

(a) A physical or electronic signature of a person authorized to act on behalf of the owner of the right allegedly infringed;
(b) Identification of each work claimed to have been infringed;
(c) Identification of the infringing material or the subject of infringing activity that is to be removed or access to which is to be disabled, including information sufficient to enable the service provider to locate the material;
(d) Information sufficient to enable the service provider to contact the complaining party, such as an electronic mail address, telephone number, or a location address at which the complaining party may be contacted;
(e) A declaration on oath that the complainant believes that the use of the material in the manner complained of is not authorized by the owner of copyright, their agent, or the law; and
(f) A statement that the information in the notification is accurate and that the complainant is authorized to act on behalf of the owner of the right allegedly infringed.

You should include the following in your notice to the service provider as per s.54(2):

(a) A physical or electronic signature of a person authorised to act on behalf of the owner of the right allegedly infringed;

(b) Identification of each work claimed to have been infringed;

(c) Identification of the infringing material or the subject of infringing activity that is to be removed or access to which is to be disabled, including information sufficient to enable the service provider to locate the material;

(d) Information sufficient to enable the service provider to contact the complaining party, such as an electronic mail address, telephone number, or a location address at which the complaining party may be contacted;

(e) A declaration on oath that the complainant believes that the use of the material in the manner complained of is not authorised by the owner of copyright, his agent or the law; and

(f) A statement that the information in the notification is accurate and that the complainant is authorised to act on behalf of the owner of the right allegedly infringed.

To avoid liability for user-generated content, a service provider must:

(a) Not have actual knowledge that the material or an activity using the material is infringing, or in the absence of such actual knowledge, not be aware of facts or circumstances from which infringing activity is apparent, and upon obtaining such knowledge or awareness, act expeditiously to remove or disable access to the material;

(b) Not receive a financial benefit directly or indirectly attributable to the infringing activity, where the service provider has the right and ability to control such activity;

(c) Respond expeditiously to remove or disable access to the material that is claimed to be infringing upon notification of infringement as provided for in section 54 of this Act; and

(d) Comply with the procedure for suspension of accounts of repeat infringers as provided in section 54 of this Act.

(See s.58)

A performer has the exclusive right to control, in relation to their performance, the acts of:

(a) fixation of their unfixed performance;  
(b) reproduction of a fixation of their performance, in any manner or form, if the:  
   (i) original fixation, other than a fixation excluded by section 68 from the necessity for obtaining the consent of the performer, was itself made without their consent;  
   (ii) reproduction is made for purposes other than those in respect of which such performer gave their consent to the making of the original fixation or of a reproduction; or  
   (iii) original fixation was made in accordance with the provisions of section 68 and the reproduction is made for purposes not covered by those provisions;  
(c) distribution to the public by sale or other transfer of ownership, of a fixation of their performance, or copies, that have not been subject to a distribution authorised by the performer;  
(d) broadcasting or communicating to the public an unfixed performance of such performer, unless the performance used in the broadcast or communication to the public is itself a broadcast performance;  
(e) renting or lending to the public or public lending of a fixation or copies of the fixation of their performance irrespective of the ownership of the copy rented or lent; and  
(f) making available to the public of their fixed performance by wire or wireless means, in a way that members of the public may access them from a place or at a time individually chosen by them.  

(s.63(1))

Yes, a performance can be used without the performer’s consent in the following situations as outlined in s.68(3):

(a) For demonstration in good faith of radio or television receivers or recording or playback equipment to clients by a dealer in those receivers or that equipment on his premises;

(b) For reproduction of short extracts from an object of performer’s rights in reports on current events, to the extent justified by that purpose;

(c) For research or private study of an object of performer’s rights kept in publicly accessible libraries, educational establishments, museums or archives, on the premises of the said institutions;

(d) For reproduction for the benefit of people with a disability, which is directly related to the disability and of a non-commercial nature, to the extent required by the disability; and

(e) For making of an ephemeral recording of an object of performer’s rights by broadcasting organisations by means of their own facilities and for their own broadcasts, provided that the recordings may be preserved for a period not more than 30 days and must be erased after their use for broadcasting, and the recordings of an exceptional documentary character may be transferred to designated archives for preservation.

To infringe on a performer's rights means to engage in certain actions without the performer's consent or authorization, which include:

(a) Doing or causing any person to do any of the acts provided in section 63 of this Act;
(b) Broadcasting a substantial part of the live performance by means of a recording, knowing or having reason to believe that it was made without the performer’s consent;
(c) Importing, for reasons other than private or domestic use, a recording of a performer’s work that is an infringing recording; or
(d) In the course of trade or business, selling or letting for hire, offering, distributing, or displaying for sale or hire, a recording of a performer’s work that is an infringing recording. 

This is outlined in s.71.

A person can violate a performer's rights by taking the following actions as outlined in s.71:

(a) Doing or causing any person to do any of the acts provided in section 63 of this Act without the performer’s consent or authorization;

(b) Broadcasting a substantial part of the live performance by means of a recording, knowing or having reason to believe that it was made without the performer’s consent;

(c) Importing, for reasons other than private or domestic use, a recording of a performer’s work that is an infringing recording; or

(d) In the course of trade or business, selling or letting for hire, offering, distributing, or displaying for sale or hire, a recording of a performer’s work that is an infringing recording.

The Commission has the authority to ensure compliance with copyright laws as outlined in s.78(3). Specifically, it can:

(a) demand for evidence of compliance from persons, public or private institutions and organisations;
(b) caution a non-compliant person or entity in writing;
(c) sanction a non-compliant person or entity by the imposition of administrative fines;
(d) institute criminal proceedings against a non-compliant person or entity;
(e) institute civil actions against the defaulting person or entity; and
(f) obtain a court warrant or order to seize or detain any book, record, document or other information storage system or database which use does not conform with the provisions of the Act or relevant regulations made under this Act.

To get your CMO approved, you need to ensure that it meets the following requirements as outlined in s.88(2):

(a) It must be incorporated as a company limited by guarantee;  
(b) Its objects must be to negotiate, grant copyright licences, collect royalties on behalf of copyright owners, and distribute such royalties;  
(c) It must represent a substantial number of owners of copyright in any category of works protected by this Act; and  
(d) It must comply with the terms and conditions prescribed by regulations made by the Commission under this Act.  

Additionally, you must apply to the Commission for approval to operate in respect of any one or more categories of works as stated in s.88(1).

The Commission checks the following criteria before approving a CMO as outlined in s.88(2):

(a) it is incorporated as a company limited by guarantee;  
(b) its objects are to negotiate, grant copyright licences, collect royalties on behalf of copyright owners and distribute such royalties;  
(c) it represents a substantial number of owners of copyright in any category of works protected by this Act; and  
(d) it complies with the terms and conditions prescribed by regulations made by the Commission under this Act.

The money that will go into the Fund includes:

(a) government statutory allocations and grants as may be provided by the Government of the Federation or appropriated by the National Assembly for payment into the Fund of the Commission;  
(b) such money as may be lent or granted to the Commission by the Federal Government, State or Local Government;  
(c) all charges, dues, fees or amount recovered by the Commission and dividends from investment;  
(d) deductions as may be approved under section 89 of this Act;  
(e) all money raised for the general purpose of the Commission with the approval of the Board; and  
(f) foreign aids and assistance from bilateral and multilateral agencies.  

This is stated in s.91(2).

The Fund established under this Act is used for the following purposes: 

(a) to perform its statutory functions under this Act and development activities in the capital projects of the Commission;  
(b) to pay approved allowances and expenses of members of the Board;  
(c) to pay salaries, allowances and other benefits of officers of the Commission;  
(d) to maintain properties acquired or vested in the Commission; and  
(e) to undertake any other activity in connection with the objects of this Act. 

(See s.92)

The types of works considered "artistic work" include, irrespective of artistic quality, any of the following works or similar works — 

(a) paintings, drawings, etchings, lithographs, woodcuts, engravings and prints;  
(b) maps, plans and diagrams;  
(c) works of sculpture;  
(d) photographs not comprised in an audiovisual work;  
(e) works of architecture in the form of building models; and  
(f) works of artistic craftsmanship, including pictorial woven tissues and articles of applied handicraft.  

This is cited as s.108(1).

Examples of what qualifies as an "artistic work" include, irrespective of artistic quality, any of the following works or similar works: 

(a) paintings, drawings, etchings, lithographs, woodcuts, engravings and prints;  
(b) maps, plans and diagrams;  
(c) works of sculpture;  
(d) photographs not comprised in an audiovisual work;  
(e) works of architecture in the form of building models; and  
(f) works of artistic craftsmanship, including pictorial woven tissues and articles of applied handicraft. 

This is cited as s.108(1).
